  "dom_id": "step-q214744",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Using the elimination method, eliminate the variable $b$ and find the solution $(a,b)$ to the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5a-b=19 \\\\ 3a+b=21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution is $($\n{{field-1}}$\\,,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$",
  "worked_solution": "Notice that the coefficient of $b$ in the first equation is $-1,$ and the coefficient of $b$ in the second equation is $1.$ \n\nSince these numbers add to zero, we can add the two equations, *eliminate* the variable $b,$ and solve the resulting equation for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5a-\\cancel{b} & =19 \\\\ 3a+\\cancel{b} & =21 \\\\ (5a+3a) & =(19+21) \\\\ 8a & =40 \\\\ a & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $a=5.$ However, we still need to find $b.$ To do this, we substitute $a=5$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a+b & =21 \\\\ 3(5)+b & =21 \\\\ 15+b & =21 \\\\ b & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=5$ and $b=6,$ which can be expressed as $(\\,5,6\\,).$",
